  <h2>What the 2022 article records</h2>
  <p>The article describes a personal search for where to learn coding and connects that curiosity to an earlier interest in games. It reflects on coding as a way to practice logic, analysis, creativity, and problem solving, while also making mathematics feel more concrete through practical use.</p>

  <h2>An early starting point</h2>
  <p>The legacy article states that coding learning began around age eight, during primary school. It also describes how pandemic-era learning shifted toward independent exploration through websites, online courses, YouTube tutorials, books, communities, and small projects.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters now</h2>
  <p>This entry is useful not because it proves advanced technical mastery, but because it captures an early learning habit: asking a question, searching broadly, learning independently, and connecting technical skills with real curiosity. That habit is relevant to the portfolio’s current direction toward science, engineering, and aerospace.</p>

